      "problem": "The center of the hyperbola $\\frac{(x+9)^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y+3)^{2}}{100}=1$ is$\\,\\,($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$",
      "worked_solution": "The equation of a horizontal hyperbola centered at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-h)^{2}}{{a}^{2}}-\\frac{(y-k)^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that the given equation can be rewritten as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-(-9))^{2}}{25}-\\frac{(y-(-3))^{2}}{100}=1\n$$\n\nIn this case, $h=-9$ and $k=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the center of the hyperbola is $(\\,-9,-3\\,).$",
